  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14096038/assets/1f6330a6e9bc0e48326982c7b7509f3e2257de994c5736c813ede93a07491e70.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?",
  "worked_solution": "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the right. The parabola has its vertex at $(0,-3)$ and passes through the point $(3,0).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(0,-3),$ we substitute $h=0$ and $k=-3$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3)^{2} & =4px.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(3,0),$ so we can substitute $x=3$ and $y=0$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3)^{2} & =4px \\\\ (0+3)^{2} & =4p(3) \\\\ 9 & =12p \\\\ p & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3)^{2} & =4px \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =4(\\frac{3}{4})x \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the right since $p=\\frac{3}{4}$ is positive.",
